WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.970
Here we have car A leaves the intersection
heading south at 20 kilometers per hour.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.650
One hour later, car B leaves the same
intersection heading east at 30 kilometers per

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.110
hour. How fast is the distance between
them changing two hours after

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.590
car A leaves? Now, I know this is a little
bit longer than our last question,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.870
but it still follows the same systematic
process. First, we have to find our knowns

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.490
and unknowns, or if you want to call them givens
and our find, the thing we have to

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.670
find. And then next, we have to find an
equation that models the relationship

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.990
between them. And then third, we take our knowns
and we plug them into that equation

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.700
and the derivative of that equation, which is
what the related rates in calculus is,

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:43.880
right?

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:49.210
So let's go through it. in our first sentence,
we have car A leaves an intersection

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.290
heading south at 20 kilometers per hour. I
don't think we have any unknowns here,

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.850
but we are given that A is going south at 20
kilometers per hour. So that's a good

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.090
piece of information that we can use to
help make our equations.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:08.320
Now, the next sentence, we have one hour later,
car B leaves the same intersection

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:13.760
heading east at 30 kilometers per hour. Again,
here we have information about car

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:18.980
B, its direction and its speed, so we're
going to underline it as a given.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.460
But this is not the only information
given to us in this sentence.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.930
While I said last time, and it's still true for
any related rates problem, usually

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.870
if we have any numbers that are important, the
reason this time one hour later is

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.910
important for us, because even though it doesn't
give information specifically about

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.230
A or B, it gives information about A
and B. It gives information

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.330
about the relationship. we know that B
is one hour after A. So

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.090
because it relates both of them together, it's a
useful piece of information for us.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.970
So we're going to underline
one hour later,

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:57.120
okay?

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:03.480
In our third sentence, we have how fast is the
distance between them changing two

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:08.680
hours after car A leaves? Now, we can
underline this whole thing as our

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:13.480
unknown because not only, because someone could
argue, okay, well, the question ends

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.760
at the distance between them changing, but it
doesn't because we have that condition

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.540
of two hours after a leaves so we're going to
underline this full sentence as our

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.380
unknown or as a thing that we have to find and
it makes sense because there's it's

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:30.190
they're asking a question right and then also
because here we're once again relating

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.350
what is happening to a and b with this two
hours after car a leaves we'll

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.070
underline it because now we know
more information about

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.410
when um or the difference between the
departure time of a and b

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.410
now the next step just like our first question
we have to find an equation that

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.670
models their relationship or relationship of the
things that matter in this equation

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.510
now it's a little bit harder for us in this
question than the last question because

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.910
usually questions when we're dealing with area
or volume the equations are easily

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.310
ready because we know the equations of area or
volume especially if we have them

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.770
right it's pretty easy to pull up here though
it's a little bit more hidden. And

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.510
usually when we're dealing with problems that
have related rates problems that we

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.710
can't tell exactly what we need to do, we should
draw the components we have and try

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.490
to figure out what's missing for
what we need to get.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.820
So here what we should do is first draw

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:38.980
the vector of a, because we know
that a's vector is

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:44.240
the velocity of it going south. So we
can draw A as an arrow

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:49.630
here, and have this be its 20
kilometers per hour, and have it

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.350
going south or down. And then we also have
B, and we can label this A.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:59.360
And then we also have B going east at 30
kilometers per hour, so we can also draw

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:03.120
what B is doing. And because it's at the same
intersection as it's mentioned, we

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.750
know that they start at the same point. And then
I can draw here 30 kilometers per

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.030
hour

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:10.860
and B.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.830
Now, if these guys were leaving at the same
time, it would be pretty easy for us to

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.670
find what C is right away because we
could just connect B and A,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.190
and then we would know what our C vector
is or what our velocity is

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:33.510
for C as well. But because they leave at
different times, this is not an accurate

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.810
representation of what's happening because
while A is going at 20 and B

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.550
is going at 30, B is leaving
one hour later.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:48.860
So what we can instead do is compare
their distances. Because since

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:53.600
we know B's velocity and A's velocity, and we
know how long they've been at this

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:58.840
velocity, we can multiply the time by
their velocity to get instead

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.810
vectors for our distance. So
here we know that

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:09.070
A, that B leaves one hour later
than A, and we're

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:14.410
calculating it two hours after A leaves.
So that means to find out what

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:19.590
our distance of A is, we'll keep that as
lowercase a, we'll say a lowercase

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.990
a equals the 20 kilometers per hour

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:29.590
multiplied by the two hours it took A.
So here the hours can cancel

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.810
out, and then we have 40 kilometers per, just
kilometers, sorry, not kilometers per

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:38.830
hour, for A. That's how far A
traveled. And for B,

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.280
we know that it traveled 30
kilometers per hour,

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:49.500
and because it was one hour later than A, it
only traveled for one hour. So we're

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.160
going to multiply this by one hour, and
then we get 30 kilometers for B.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.560
Now, if we draw A and B, we
can draw A here.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.500
It's going to be a little bit longer than B, so
I'll make it a little bit longer.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:08.460
Here, we're going to draw 40. And
here, we will draw our 30,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:14.790
a little bit longer. Then we can figure out the
relationship between the distance

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.800
of A and B. So we know, at least
we might not know

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:23.980
how fast the distance between them is
changing, but we can find out just the

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:29.460
distance between them, because the
distance C here will be 40, or A

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:34.260
squared, because 40 is equal to A, plus B
squared. So we can now have a relationship

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:38.720
that models their distances, which is the
Pythagorean theorem. We have A squared

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:43.720
plus B squared equals C squared,
okay? And because

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:48.480
this models our distance, we know
that the derivative

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:53.940
of the distance is time. So if we take

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:58.880
the derivative of this whole
equation, implicitly

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:04.380
doing it with Leibniz notation, we can
derive all of them with

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:09.680
respect to time. So we can do d of a
squared over t over dt

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.390
plus d of b squared over dt

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:20.200
equals d of c squared over dt.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:26.550
Now, using chain and
Leibniz, we can get 2a

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.500
da over dt plus 2b db over dt

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:38.020
equals 2c dc over dt, just like we did
for our last one when we were

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:43.340
doing the, when we were deriving
surface area equals 4 pi

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:44.140
r squared, right?

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:50.610
So now we actually know A as 40. So
we know this component.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:51.910
We know B.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:58.070
But we don't yet know C. But we also know
the rates of A and B, because we

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.610
have them from up here. And these are the rates
for A and B, right? As I labeled

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.530
them capital and capital. So the only two
things we're missing right now is our

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:12.960
c and our dc over dt. And from our
Pythagorean theorem,

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:17.220
we can find out c as well. So let's quickly go
find out c and then plug them all in

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:22.570
to find out the rate of c. So here
we can go and we can do,

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:26.690
that means c equals the square root of
a squared plus b squared.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:32.670
So C equals square root of 40

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:37.590
squared plus 30 squared. So C
will then equal

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:39.490
1,600 plus 900.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:44.940
And then let's quickly, that'll be, actually,
we don't need to do a calculator.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:50.440
That'll be [√2,500], which would be 50. So C
equals 50. So now we can plug

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:53.460
everything into here and solve
for our dc over dt,

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:58.500
because this would be our C. And of course,
this is in kilometers, right?

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:05.370
So plugging everything into our principal
equation, we have 2 times 40

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:06.710
for A, 40 kilometers,

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:13.850
times 20 for our rate of A, 20
kilometers per hour,

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:19.380
plus 2 times B, which is 30 kilometers,

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:23.120
times 30 kilometers per hour

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.170
equals 2 times 50 kilometers, which is

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:30.570
our C,

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:36.380
times our unknown, which is
dc over dt. Now

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:41.000
rearranging, we get DC over

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:47.040
DT is equal to 2

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:52.280
times 40 times 20. It's the same as 40
squared, which would be 1

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:52.820
,600.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:59.160
And this kilometers is going to be kilometers
squared over h because we have 2

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:04.260
kilometers, so it's kilometers per hour squared.
And then here we have 30 times 30,

